WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.680
If we think about global warming, which
we can all agree is a dire problem and a

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.900
problem that's getting worse. The instant
I start thinking about it as a type of

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:14.340
pollution—as pollution caused by
greenhouse gases—the problem becomes

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.720
clearer to me. And I say this, I don't say this to minimize the problem or

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:23.369
trivialize the problem at all. All I'm
trying to do is categorize the problem

00:00:23.369 --> 00:00:27.930
properly. Because once you think about
greenhouse gases as pollution, then you

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:32.279
understand the toolkit for addressing
them. And in particular, the toolkit that

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:37.500
works really well for other kinds of
atmospheric pollution is: put a price on

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.610
the pollution. Make it a thing that
businesses have to spend on like they

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:45.809
spend for every other material out there, and then watch them try not to spend

00:00:45.809 --> 00:00:51.270
money on it. Watch them flee from that
cost. This is what drove down our air

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:55.890
pollution levels so much. I imagine a lot
of us in this room have at least two

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:03.000
friends who are economists. If you have
at least two economist friends, you

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.580
realize that economists agree on nothing. And your two friends, or any number, will

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.420
just fight endlessly about any topic in economics. I say this to bring up the

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:17.790
fact that economists are not fighting
about the most powerful tool to address

00:01:17.790 --> 00:01:21.750
global warming, to address greenhouse gas pollution. This is an open letter arguing

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.380
for a carbon tax. And in particular, a carbon tax where the government doesn't

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:29.460
keep the money; it just sends it right back out to the citizens of the country,

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:34.290
called a carbon dividend. This letter was
originally signed by a few dozen

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:39.420
economists. By now, the open letter has
been signed by more than 3,500 prominent

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:44.899
economists. This has never happened
before in the history of the discipline.

00:01:44.899 --> 00:01:49.710
They would not sign a letter about the
sun rising in the east. But the the level

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:55.560
of unanimity here about the the most
powerful policy tool that we have to

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:59.610
combat global warming is just incredibly clear. And it's very frustrating that

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:04.110
we're not doing this, we're not even
taking it very seriously. Here's where we

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:08.129
left our whales in the early 1970s.
That's where the blue whales were. Here's

00:02:08.129 --> 00:02:12.010
where they are now.
They're not all the way back, and they

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:15.790
might never get back to those previous
levels. But one phrase that I learned

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:19.420
from Stewart [Brand] the last time he and I were
together is one that I just carry around

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.900
with me all the time.
Stewart didn't invent this, but he said it

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:29.050
and I thought it was fantastic. It's:
'Nature bats last.' And if you carry that

00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.380
idea around, you just come to the
conclusion that, look: we make mistakes

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:35.950
and we shouldn't make them, but if we can
get out of the way, if we can leave, if we

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:40.540
can leave the field, let the oceans
repopulate, don't hunt there anymore, stop

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:44.800
farming that land, and let it go back to
nature, nature can rejuvenate. It's

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.780
astonishingly resilient. Nature actually
bats last.

